WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.200
 Hello and welcome to our lecture on the CDC 6504 architecture. So as you'll soon see, this is a

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:12.560
 blend of two names so that I can avoid confusion with the actual processor that you're simulating.

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:21.200
 So the processor that we're simulating is the MOS Technology 6502. There are many microprocessors

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:28.080
 that have deep historical significance. The 6502 is a very significant processor in terms of the

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:34.960
 history of CPU architecture and its evolution. It was launched in 1975 and the key to it was low

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:42.000
 cost of manufacture. It was simple. It only had four thousand transistors. Remember the Intel 4004

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:47.440
 had two thousand three hundred transistors. This ran into megahertz and they kept the price low at

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:54.080
 twenty five dollars. So it just allowed a whole series of hobbyist computers like the Apple One.

00:00:54.080 --> 00:01:00.560
 That was a hobbyist computer. And so it sort of just opened up a whole bunch of things.

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:07.120
 And so the problem is, is that if I name my emulator the 6502 emulator, it would get lost

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:16.080
 in all of the confusion. So I changed it to the CDC 6504 to pay homage to the first computer that

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:23.200
 I ever used in the 1970s from Control Data Corporation. I learned Fortran on it. I learned

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:27.200
 assembly language on it. I learned machine language on it. And I have a picture here

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:32.240
 of a running version of this computer at the Living Computer Museum in Seattle,

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:38.160
 Washington that sadly is now closed after Paul Allen passed away. But I have a video that I

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:46.720
 will share with you that is a video of a running CDC 6500 from a couple of years ago. I further was

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:56.000
 lucky to meet an important influencer in the CDC 6500 history, Niklaus Wirth, and he invented

00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:04.000
 Pascal. I used Pascal. I supported the Pascal compiler. It was a really important part of my

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:11.760
 own sort of education was to build compilers. And so I'm going to show you an artifact from

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:17.840
 that time period. So this is from-- I don't know if you can see it. Let's see if you can see it

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:26.960
 here. This is from-- yeah, there you go-- 1980. This was a one-year project in computer science

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:34.720
 where I-- this is assembly language from the CDC 6500. This is a compiler and an optimizer,

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:40.880
 and so it was written in Fortran. These are line printers. Let's see if I can find something

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:47.040
 interesting to show you with all kinds of documentation. And so we wrote all this stuff

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:54.480
 in 1979 and 1980. It was a two-student project, and I have kept it for all this time.

00:02:54.480 --> 00:03:04.880
 There we go. So that's an artifact from the CDC 6500. And again, I named the CDC 6504

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:11.280
 just so I had something to search on. So this is the architecture of the CDC 6504.

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:16.000
 And the first thing you have to understand is the registers. And when you start using this

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:20.800
 interactively online, you'll be reading these registers. So the most important register, the

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:27.040
 what next register, is the program counter, also known as the PC. As we've seen in some earlier

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:31.680
 lectures, there's the current instruction register, which is important. And it often is not shown on

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:37.920
 architecture diagrams, even though it's very important. The accumulator. So the CDC 6504 and

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:45.200
 the 6502 is an accumulator-based system. And that's sort of like the most power. They have one single

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:52.320
 most powerful register, and that is the accumulator. As you do operations, they set flags after every

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:57.840
 operation so that you can do if tests. So was the last result zero? Was the last result negative?

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:02.640
 It has two more registers that are a little less powerful than the accumulator called the X and Y

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:08.880
 registers. And on the emulator itself that I've built, it shows the instructions in base two,

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:14.320
 which makes it easier to decode. And the data memory will be shown in hex, given that most of

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:21.840
 the time we're just going to be putting characters into the data memory. So we program the CDC 6504

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:27.280
 in machine language. And so there's some really good documentation that is that I built with the

00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:33.520
 help of AI. So this is a similar language for the clear instruction, clear X, increment X,

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:40.160
 et cetera, et cetera. So we'll cover these in much more detail. So let's start right away

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:47.600
 with our first program. So you can kind of go back. So our program is going to compute one plus one

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:57.520
 equals two in the X register. So the PC tells the system where to retrieve the next instruction from.

00:04:57.520 --> 00:05:01.760
 So when the program, when the computer starts, before we start it running,

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:08.480
 the PC starts at zero. And so the first instruction is one, one, one, zero, zero, one, zero,

00:05:08.480 --> 00:05:14.400
 which is clearing the X register. Now the system starts at zeros in there, but it's probably smart

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:19.920
 just to clear it anywhere, anyway. Then it has two increment instructions, and then it has an all

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:27.600
 zeros instruction, which says stop. Okay. So let's walk this through. So when the first execution of

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:33.760
 the first instruction is done, well, that sets the X register to zero. But then the side effect of

00:05:33.760 --> 00:05:40.960
 that is to increment the PC register to one and then run that instruction. And so when that

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:47.120
 instruction runs, we increment X. And so you can see that X is now incremented to one. But then once

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:53.920
 that instruction is run, then it moves to the O2 instruction. And that is also an increment,

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:59.600
 which it reads, and then it increments X to two. And then it runs to the last instruction, which is

00:05:59.600 --> 00:06:06.720
 a break. And then that stops our program. So it runs, runs three instructions, and then stops.

00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:14.400
 So at this point, you might say to yourself, "Oh, wait a second. This seems familiar."

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:23.760
 Clearing a register and incrementing a register, and then stopping, that's something we've done

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:30.640
 before. Literally, in previous lectures, you have built a two instruction computer with a latch,

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:37.440
 and an adder, and a clock. And so you can say, "Oh, well, what is this?" Well, the register is

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:44.320
 like the latch. The adder is the thing that does the increment for us, because the latch's output

00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:50.640
 goes into the adder, and we've hard-coded a one to the other input of the adder. And then we have a

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:56.960
 clock that decides when things are going to run. And then we have an instruction, which is either

00:06:56.960 --> 00:07:03.600
 zero or one. This is a one-bit computer, right? And the thing that I complained about several times

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:08.080
 in previous lectures is that it's not reading instructions from memory. What we just got done

00:07:08.080 --> 00:07:12.160
 doing is reading instructions from memory. And then there's the decode logic, which is always

00:07:12.160 --> 00:07:16.560
 a difficult thing for students to understand, where it is looking at literally the bits of

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:24.240
 the instruction and using AND gates and OR gates, actually enabling or disabling parts of the

00:07:24.240 --> 00:07:30.560
 processor. So the increment instruction, or the loading into the latch based on the clock,

00:07:30.560 --> 00:07:37.520
 is turned off based on the instruction. So the decoding, and if you look at the decoding of a

00:07:37.520 --> 00:07:46.000
 typical microprocessor, it's like a whole city block, right? And so the instructions in a real

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:53.520
 computer sitting in memory, the decoding has to look at all eight of those bits in our 6502,

00:07:53.520 --> 00:08:00.240
 and it has to send all those bits to various places to turn on and off elements of the registers

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:05.840
 and elements of the arithmetic logic unit. And so we had it, this is eight-bit instructions,

00:08:05.840 --> 00:08:10.960
 which means we have a whole bunch of things that we can do and control. Now,

00:08:10.960 --> 00:08:18.400
 if you think about it, at this point, if you had enough time,

00:08:18.400 --> 00:08:28.560
 you could build a CDC 6504 and manufacturing capability. Because if you go to that simple CPU

00:08:29.360 --> 00:08:37.040
 and that simple CPU, the latch, the adder, and the clock can be mapped to transistors,

00:08:37.040 --> 00:08:43.360
 because underneath them they're transistors, and we covered that. And then we showed you how to make

00:08:43.360 --> 00:08:49.520
 VLSI logic design, where you can lay out in a two-dimensional grid different materials that

00:08:49.520 --> 00:08:57.360
 cause transistors, right? And then all you would need is a billion dollar personal manufacturing

00:08:57.360 --> 00:09:06.000
 facility to turn those transistor layouts into chips. And then you could cut those chips up,

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:14.240
 and then you could manufacture a 6502. Now, the goal of this course is not for you to go

00:09:14.240 --> 00:09:20.000
 and build a two billion dollar microprocessor fabrication facility, but to understand that

00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:29.600
 really you know the essence of the entire process to build a complex CPU and produce it. It's

00:09:29.600 --> 00:09:35.200
 possible, and you understand the basics of it. So up next we're just going to start going into

00:09:35.200 --> 00:09:48.160
 more detail of the entire machine language.

